  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ADB Cities Dev. Initi. (CDIA),Phase 2, 2013-17 - Cities Development Initiative for Asia, Phase 2</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">ADB Cities Dev. Initi. (CDIA),Phase 2, 2013-17</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Cities Development Initiative for Asia (CDIA) aims to contribute to the promotion of sustainable and equitable urban development, leading to improved environmental and living conditions for all in Asian and Pacific cities.&#xa0;To achieve this, CDIA bridges the gap between strategic city development planning and urban policies, services, and infrastructure project preparation.&#xa0;It uses a demand-driven approach to support&#xa0;infrastructure projects that emphasize poverty reduction, environmental improvement,&#xa0;climate change mitigation or adaptation, and good governance.
The operational objectives of CDIA, are to improve urban infrastructure and services management through:
- Assisting medium-size Asian cities to prepare sustainable and bankable quality infrastructure projects so to bridge the gap between their development plans and the implementation of priority projects;
- Linking infrastructure projects with funding sources; and
- Strengthening individual and organizational capacities of stakeholders for developing sustainable and bankable projects and for ensuring financing.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">The Cities Development Initiative for Asia (CDIA) contribution,Phase  2, 2013-2017, facilitates the identification and preparation of       projects for financing, facilitate international dialogue and         networking among city administrations and stakeholders, and also      provide training and advice to decision-makers from Asian cities and  urban areas.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Cities Development Initiative for Asia (CDIA) contribution,Phase  2, 2013-2017, facilitatse the identification and preparation of       projects for financing, facilitate international dialogue and         networking among city administrations and stakeholders, and also      provide training and advice to decision-makers from Asian cities and  urban areas.</narrative>
  </description>
